### Summary

As the maintainer, I want an independent review by **Fable 5.2** of SwiftHFR against Apple's current UI/UX guidance, so that every gap with the platform is found, sorted and tracked before release. The guidance is the HIG plus the WWDC24, WWDC25 and WWDC26 sessions, reconciled chronologically, plus UIKit. (Operator, 2026-11-13.)

### On hold

Held by the operator's schedule: an end-of-M4 review, started by the orchestrator when the M4 lots are merged, like #774. ([decision](https://github.com/ezzz/HFRnow/issues/368))

### When

End of M4. This review is read-only, so it can run **in parallel with #774** (performance). Its findings that change shared components are fixed under the grouped-claim rule of #836: one shared component, one pass.

### Method

1. **Build the reference, chronologically.** Gather the UI/UX recommendations of:
   - the Human Interface Guidelines (current pages);
   - the WWDC24, WWDC25 and WWDC26 design and UI sessions (UIKit, SwiftUI, Liquid Glass and new design system, navigation, bars, tab bars, sheets, menus, typography, accessibility, iPad and iPhone Duo layouts);
   - the UIKit and SwiftUI documentation for the behaviours they describe.

   **When several sources cover the same subject, keep only the most recent recommendation**, and note what it supersedes. Every rule cites its source: HIG page URL, or WWDC session URL with its timestamp. Nothing comes from memory (AGENTS.md §3 rule 1 applies to Apple too).
2. **Audit the app** (`App/SwiftHFR`) against that reference, screen by screen:
   - lists and rows;
   - navigation and top bars (see #865: the title scrolls away);
   - tab bar;
   - reader chrome;
   - composer;
   - sheets, alerts and toasts (see #864: auto-dismiss);
   - settings;
   - empty, loading and error states;
   - iPad split view and iPhone Duo;
   - Dynamic Type, VoiceOver, contrast, reduced motion.

   Read the code and, where needed, ask for a Mac capture: do not guess the rendering.
3. **Check every finding against legacy parity first.** M4 is legacy parity (`87fe0d1c`). A gap that legacy shares is still filed, but marked "(b) evolution, M5" unless the HIG makes it a defect (accessibility, broken platform behaviour).

### Output

1. One comment here: the reference, rule by rule with its source and the rules it superseded, then the gaps ranked by severity.
2. **One issue per gap, or per coherent group of gaps on one shared component.** Each issue has:
   - `### Summary`;
   - the rule and its source;
   - the screens and `file:line`;
   - the expected behaviour;
   - M4 parity or M5 evolution;
   - the definition of done;
   - the routing (AGENTS.md §6);
   - every axis label, **plus the end-of-M4 review label of this review** (see below).
3. No code change in this issue.

### End-of-M4 review labels (operator idea, 2026-11-13)

Every issue opened by an end-of-M4 review carries one label naming the review that found it. At the end of M4, the gaps found by each review can then be counted, qualified, sorted and triaged. The label scheme is in the orchestrator's proposal on #368, and the labels are added to `.github/labels.yml` before the first review starts. For this review: the HIG / WWDC label.

### Definition of done

- [ ] The reference is posted, chronological and with sources.
- [ ] Every screen family is audited.
- [ ] Every gap is filed as above, with this review's label.
- [ ] This issue is closed with the list and the counts per severity and per M4/M5.

Refs #774, #775, #836, #326, #865, #864.


